// THE DISPATCH KEY IS THE OP, NOT THE EFFECT. Two handlers cover one
// effect's DISJOINT op sets — `ha` answers `a`, `hb` answers `b` — and both
// are installed. Resolving `b` must walk PAST the innermost handler, because
// covering the effect is not answering the op.
//
// Born RED 2026-09-09 against the pin that keyed the lexical walk on the
// effect: `mentl check` reported ZERO diagnostics and the emit wrote
// `(call $op_ha_b)`, an arm `ha` does not have, so the ASSEMBLER was the
// first thing in the chain to object. The hazard was NAMED in the tree
// already — LDirectPerform's own comment says "an ename key mis-resolved the
// split-effect pair" — and the response taken there had been to key the
// singleton walk by HANDLER name instead, which cannot answer an ambiguous
// op at all and is the whole of the eta-wrapper ceiling.
//
// Nothing was added to fix it: the lowering already lays each handler
// group's arms dense over the effect's op slots, with a gap where the handler
// does not implement one, and the runtime record's arm region leaves those
// slots zero. Both sides could always answer "does this handler declare this
// op"; only the question was wrong. Expected: 11 + 22 = 33.
effect Two {
  a(n: Int) -> Int
  b(n: Int) -> Int
}

handler ha {
  a(n) => resume(n + 1),
}

handler hb {
  b(n) => resume(n + 2),
}

fn main() = a(10) + b(20) ~> ha ~> hb
